{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgP1u1SvCv9GG65LtohSJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I updated both binary-reverse-engineering files, and nothing else. One problem from today is still open: the day25 solver has a bug that made Mastery's first submission incorrect. It is recorded but not fixed, because `solve.py` is outside my two files.\n\n1. **Technique log** (`reference/techniques/binary-reverse-engineering.md`, 239 lines):\n   - **Day 25 entry:** the artifact was a vulnerable/corrected pair of compiled Clojure jars. The config blob sits in the namespace-loader class (`<ns>__init.class`), not the class named as the entry point. The only changed function was `parse-frame`: the vulnerable build reads item 4 of a list without checking its length (an out-of-bounds read), and the fix adds that check. It is reached only when two environment variables (`HUNTRESS_HOST_MODEL`, `HUNTRESS_TRIGGER_FAULT`) are set a certain way. As with day 24, none of this was asked; the questions covered only headers and the config blob.\n   - **Results:** single solve CORRECT at T+80 s (about 69 s of that was the download), 0 wrong, no new code. Time Trial 10.5 s, first try.\n   - **Mastery:** the first submission was incorrect, with 55 answers wrong across 5 of the 9 sets. The solver caches per-file results keyed on Python object identity (`id(bytes)`). With 9 sets, Python reused memory addresses, so some files were answered with another day's file's results. Per-set retries plus a live worker reached CORRECT about 2 minutes after start.\n   - **Sections revised:**\n     - \"How challenges have looked\": vulnerability-research arc, fourth \"new language on an old runtime\", 300 questions and 9 sets.\n     - Recognition cues: new Clojure row.\n     - Variant axes: Clojure config location; new domain and URL words in the timed modes; a cache-identity rule under Mastery packaging.\n     - Pitfalls and answer conventions: the cache bug, proving bounds errors statically, `jar` confirmed for Clojure.\n   - I shortened the day 22–24 entries and merged duplicate rows so the file stays about the same length.\n\n2. **Coverage atlas** (`reference/coverage/binary-reverse-engineering.md`):\n   - Added a day 25 summary and an updated forecast: another bug class per day, possibly moving to native twins, with Groovy as the likely day 26 language.\n   - Concept rows 7, 93, 113, 114 and 115 are marked `seen day25`. New rows 119–121 cover bug-site reachability, Clojure jars and Groovy.\n   - The worker quick-reference is 58 lines (limit 60), and the gaps JSON is valid with the same keys.\n\n3. **Readiness gaps** now have 12 unbuilt `prebuild` gaps (only 5 remained before; at least 8 required). Priority 1 is:\n   - `solver-member-cache-identity`: a note, not a prebuild, with the file locations and the fix.\n   - `clojure-namespace-facts`\n   - `groovy-script-class-facts`\n   - `bug-reachability-gating-facts`\n   - `jvm-twin-diff-gensym-normalization`\n   - `crash-exploitability-facts` and `symbolic-input-solver`, both raised from priority 2.\n\n   New at priority 2: `native-bounds-check-twins`, `next-bug-class-twin-fixtures`, `standalone-classfile-member`. I lowered already-built gaps that are unlikely in the next three days to priority 2.\n\n**Before day 26's timed runs:** change the solver's caches (`_SHA`, `_ZIPS`, `_ISJAR`, `_FACTS`, `_CFG` in `solve.py`) to key on the delivery and member name, or the sha256, instead of `id()`. The `solver-member-cache-identity` gap has the line numbers and the regression case to rerun (`cases/variant-mastery-f395c0c5`)."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1718,"cache_read_input_tokens":223634,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1718},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standar